Before you open the box (and "measure" the system), the radioactive particle's
wavefunction is uncollapsed.  It is neither decayed nor undecayed, it's both.
Because of the connection between particle decay and cat death, the cat's 
wavefunction is also uncollapsed: it's neither dead nor alive.  Only when you
open the box and look does the cat collapse into one of the two states.

If the box is transparent, or you have some other means of looking inside, that
counts as "measuring."  You can ask if the cat is an observer; this brings up
the general question of "what is an observer"? -- I.e., if you do the 
experiment, then before you tell me the results, as far as I'm concerned both
the cat and you are uncollapsed.

The main point of the story is that "Physics is weird" -- common sense from the
ordinary world doesn't always apply.  BTW, I've never heard the "tripwire"
version of the story, and I'm not sure I see what value it adds..

Now in NetHack, things are a little different.  Container items can only
contain "things," and though corpses are things, monsters aren't.  So the box
can only contain a dead cat.

I guess the only way to "correct" this (if we're going to belabor the joke)
would be do create "on the fly" either a cat or cat corpse the first time the
box is opened (like djinnis coming from bottles).

